WEBMASTERID SDK LICENSE AGREEMENT
Version 1.0

This WebmasterID SDK License Agreement (the "Agreement") is between HELPERG LLC, a
Wyoming, United States limited liability company with a registered address at 30 N Gould St
Ste N, Sheridan, WY 82801, United States ("HELPERG", "we", "us"), and the
individual or organisation that downloads, integrates or distributes the SDK Binaries
("you").

1. Acceptance

1.1 By downloading the SDK Binaries, adding them to an application, or distributing an
application that contains them, you accept this Agreement. If you do not accept it, do not
download, integrate or distribute the SDK Binaries.

1.2 If you accept this Agreement on behalf of an organisation, you represent that you have
authority to bind it, and "you" means that organisation.

1.3 This Agreement is published with each release of the SDK Binaries: in the public
distribution repository for iOS, and at the address named in the Maven metadata of the
Android artifacts.

1.4 Sending data to the Service through the SDK Binaries is use of the Service, which the
Service Terms govern. This Agreement governs the SDK Binaries themselves (section 23.1).

2. Definitions

2.1 "SDK Binaries" means the compiled WebmasterID software development kit components
that HELPERG publishes under this Agreement, together with the interface, metadata,
privacy-manifest, notice and documentation files published with them. They are:
(a) for iOS, WebmasterID.xcframework and WebmasterIDStoreKit.xcframework, version 1.2.0
and later;
(b) for Android, the Maven artifacts com.webmasterid:webmasterid-core,
com.webmasterid:webmasterid-android and com.webmasterid:webmasterid-googleplay, version
0.2.0 and later; and
(c) any other WebmasterID SDK component in binary form that HELPERG publishes with this
Agreement.

2.2 "Your App" means an application that you develop, that includes the SDK Binaries, and
whose primary functionality is not the SDK Binaries themselves.

2.3 "End User" means a person who obtains or uses Your App.

2.4 "App Store" means the Apple App Store, Google Play and any other channel through which
applications are distributed to End Users, including test channels such as TestFlight and
Google Play testing tracks, and an organisation's private or enterprise app distribution.

2.5 "Build Processing" means automated processing of Your App, including the SDK
Binaries, by your build tools or by an App Store, for the purpose of building, packaging,
optimising, signing, encrypting, thinning or distributing Your App. Examples include
compiling and linking, code shrinking, optimisation and obfuscation such as R8 or ProGuard,
app thinning, and re-signing by an App Store.

2.6 "Service" means the WebmasterID hosted service operated by HELPERG, including the
endpoints that receive data from the SDK Binaries.

2.7 "Service Terms" means the WebmasterID Terms of Service published at
https://webmasterid.com/terms, as they are amended in accordance with their own terms.

2.8 "DPA" means a data processing agreement between you and HELPERG that both of you have
signed. Where no such agreement exists, references to a DPA have no effect.

2.9 "Documentation" means the integration and privacy documentation that HELPERG publishes
with the SDK Binaries in their distribution channels, including their README files, privacy
manifests and third-party notices.

2.10 "Feedback" means suggestions, comments or ideas about the SDK Binaries that you
choose to give HELPERG.

2.11 "Marks" means the names "WebmasterID" and "HELPERG", and any logos, trademarks or
service marks of HELPERG.

2.12 "Customer Proxy" means a server or gateway, operated by you or by a service provider
for you, that receives data from the SDK Binaries in Your App and forwards it to the Service.

2.13 "Private Build Cache" means a cache, mirror or artifact repository that is not
publicly accessible and that you, your contractors or your build services use to build your
applications — for example a Maven proxy, a Swift Package Manager cache or a CI cache.

2.14 "Dependency-Only Wrapper" means software that you distribute to other developers —
for example a plugin for React Native, Unity, .NET MAUI or Capacitor — that does not contain
the SDK Binaries but declares a dependency on them, so that each developer's build obtains
the SDK Binaries from HELPERG's distribution channels.

2.15 "Third-Party Components" means software, code or other material in or used by the SDK
Binaries that HELPERG does not own, including open-source components, code that compilers
copy from standard libraries into the SDK Binaries, and platform software of Apple, Google
or others.

2.16 "MIT Releases" means the versions of the WebmasterID Swift SDK distributed under the
MIT License, including source releases 1.0.0, 1.0.1 and 1.1.0, and any other copy of
WebmasterID code that you received under the MIT License.

3. License grant

3.1 Subject to this Agreement, HELPERG grants you a non-exclusive, worldwide,
non-transferable (except under section 23.4) license, for as long as this Agreement is in
effect, to:
(a) use and reproduce the SDK Binaries to develop, build, test and support Your App;
(b) integrate the SDK Binaries into Your App, including through Build Processing;
(c) distribute the SDK Binaries to End Users in object-code form only as part of Your App,
directly or through any App Store, including the App Store's own processing, re-signing and
delivery of Your App; and
(d) permit End Users to use the SDK Binaries only as part of their use of Your App.

3.2 You may permit your employees and your contractors, including agencies that build Your App
for you, to exercise the rights in section 3.1 on your behalf. You are responsible for their
compliance with this Agreement.

3.3 You may keep copies of the SDK Binaries in Private Build Caches.

3.4 HELPERG charges no licence fee for the license in section 3.1. That license does not
include use of the Service free of charge: sending data to the Service, and HELPERG's
receipt, processing and display of it, are use of the Service, which the Service Terms and
the plan that applies to your WebmasterID account govern, including that plan's fees and
limits.

4. Use with the Service

4.1 The SDK Binaries are designed to send data from Your App to the Service. Your use of the
Service, including an app property and the data the SDK Binaries send to it, is governed by
the Service Terms and, where one exists, a DPA.

4.2 You may configure the SDK Binaries to send data only to: (a) the Service; (b) a Customer
Proxy; or (c) for development and testing, an endpoint that HELPERG operates for you, or an
endpoint that you operate only to inspect or test the traffic of the SDK Binaries.

4.3 You are responsible for a Customer Proxy and for data while it is in a Customer Proxy.
HELPERG is not responsible for data that a Customer Proxy alters, delays, loses or retains,
and data reaches the Service only when the Customer Proxy delivers it.

5. Restrictions

Except as this Agreement expressly permits, or as applicable law permits despite this
limitation (section 8.1(a)), you will not, and will not permit anyone else to:
(a) distribute, sell, rent, lease or lend the SDK Binaries on their own, or otherwise than as
section 6 allows;
(b) sublicense the SDK Binaries, except as section 7 allows;
(c) modify, adapt or translate the SDK Binaries, except through Build Processing;
(d) decompile, disassemble or reverse engineer the SDK Binaries, except as section 8 allows;
(e) remove, alter or obscure any copyright, license, attribution or third-party notice in or
published with the SDK Binaries;
(f) circumvent any technical limitation in the SDK Binaries, including their consent and
data-minimisation controls; or
(g) use the SDK Binaries in violation of applicable law, including laws on privacy, consent
and electronic communications, or in violation of the rules of an App Store through which
Your App is distributed, to the extent those rules concern the SDK Binaries or the data they
collect.

6. Redistribution

6.1 You may not distribute the SDK Binaries on their own, re-host or mirror them publicly,
publish them in a public package registry, or include them in any software development kit,
library, framework, plugin, wrapper or developer tool that you distribute to other
developers.

6.2 Section 6.1 does not restrict:
(a) distribution as part of Your App under section 3.1;
(b) copies in Private Build Caches under section 3.3;
(c) software that HELPERG itself publishes for developers, such as the webmasterid_flutter
package, which retrieves the SDK Binaries from HELPERG's channels when Your App is built; or
(d) a Dependency-Only Wrapper, provided that it does not contain, re-host or modify the SDK
Binaries, does not suggest that HELPERG made or endorses it (section 13), and tells its users
that the SDK Binaries it obtains are licensed under this Agreement. Each developer whose build
obtains the SDK Binaries through a Dependency-Only Wrapper is a licensee under this Agreement.
Your own code in the wrapper is licensed under the terms you choose.

6.3 HELPERG may permit, in writing, other software that redistributes the SDK Binaries.

7. Sublicensing

You may not sublicense the SDK Binaries. Distribution under section 3.1(c), use by End Users
under section 3.1(d), and use by your employees and contractors under section 3.2 are not
sublicensing for the purpose of this section and need no further permission. Nor is granting
an App Store, for the SDK Binaries as part of Your App, the permissions its distribution
agreement with you requires — for example to copy, review, analyse, process, re-sign and
deliver Your App — which section 3.1(c) allows.

8. Reverse engineering

8.1 Section 5(d) does not apply:
(a) to the extent that applicable law gives you a right that cannot be excluded or limited by
contract — for example, in the European Union, the rights to observe, study or test the
functioning of a program, to make a back-up copy, and to decompile it to achieve
interoperability (Articles 5 and 6 of Directive 2009/24/EC). Nothing in this Agreement
excludes or limits such a right; any conditions that the law attaches to it apply;
(b) to Build Processing;
(c) to inspecting the behaviour of the SDK Binaries within Your App for debugging,
integration, performance, privacy, security or compliance purposes — for example reading
their logs and network traffic, using symbol files that HELPERG provides, or reading their
published interface files and privacy manifests; or
(d) to good-faith security research, provided you report any vulnerability to HELPERG
privately as described in the security policy published with the SDK Binaries, and do not
disclose it publicly before HELPERG has fixed it or 90 days have passed since your
report, whichever is earlier, unless the law requires earlier disclosure.

8.2 The SDK Binaries contain no secret, and compiled code can be inspected. This section
governs what you may do contractually; it is not a security measure.

